---
usage: new_note.py [-h] --segment {career,goals,personal,work} [--area AREA]
                   [--project PROJECT] [--repo REPO]
                   [--type {feedback,learning,skill}] --title TITLE
                   [--tags TAGS] --body BODY [--links LINKS]
                   [--aliases ALIASES] [--supersedes SUPERSEDES]
                   [--reason REASON]

Create one atomic note in the second-brain vault.

Enforces the vault's conventions so every note (however it gets created —
main-thread, recap subagent, or the user by hand) ends up in the same shape:
a timestamp-ID filename that never changes (the permalink), frontmatter with
a stable schema, and a short body.

Usage:
    new_note.py --segment work --project payments-api \
        --title "Docker healthcheck timeout on staging" \
        --tags docker,timeout \
        --body "Healthcheck was timing out at 5s under load.\nFix: raised to 15s in docker-compose.yml." \
        --links 202608301090-staging-env-vars

Segments: personal, work, career, goals
  --area NAME      only with --segment personal: files under 00-Personal/<area>/
                   (any name works, but Health/Relationships/Home-Finance/Hobbies/Travel
                   are the established vocabulary — see references/vault-structure.md)
  --project NAME   only with --segment work: files under 01-Work/<project>/
  --repo NAME      only with --segment work: files under 01-Work/codebase-wiki/<repo>/
                   (--project and --repo are mutually exclusive)
  --type {skill,feedback,learning}
                   only with --segment career: files under 02-Career/<skills|feedback|learning-log>/
  --segment goals notes are always flat under 03-Goals/ (no subfolder flag) —
                   goals notes must stay thin: target + status + links out to evidence
                   elsewhere, never the evidence itself.

Revising a decision (never overwrite):
  --supersedes ID-SLUG   the id-slug (or bare numeric id) of an existing note this
                         one revises. The OLD note's content is never touched — it
                         only gets a `superseded_by:` pointer added to its frontmatter.
                         The new note gets a matching `supersedes:` pointer plus a
                         "Supersedes: [[...]]" line in its body. Use --reason to record
                         why, so the old (wrong) decision and the correction are both
                         visible and linked, instead of one silently replacing the other.

If the vault is a git repo (it is, by default), every run of this script also commits
the change — so even outside the supersede convention, `git log -p <file>` gives a
full diff history of any note.

Folder linking: Obsidian can't link to a folder, only to a file. So every folder gets
a `<folder-name>.md` index note living inside it — this script creates any missing
ones automatically, all the way up to the vault root (whose index is `Welcome.md`).
Every note this script creates gets a `Parent: [[...]]` line pointing at its
containing folder's index, and every folder index has its own `Parent:` line
pointing at ITS parent's index — so following Parent links from any note always
reaches Welcome.md eventually.

Prints the created path and the wikilink target to use from other notes.

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --segment {career,goals,personal,work}
  --area AREA           personal only: subfolder under 00-Personal/<area>/
  --project PROJECT     work only: subfolder under 01-Work/<project>/
  --repo REPO           work only: subfolder under 01-Work/codebase-
                        wiki/<repo>/
  --type {feedback,learning,skill}
                        career only: skill, feedback, or learning
  --title TITLE
  --tags TAGS           comma-separated, no #
  --body BODY           note body; use \n for newlines
  --links LINKS         comma-separated existing note filenames (no .md) for a
                        Related line
  --aliases ALIASES     comma-separated alternate titles
  --supersedes SUPERSEDES
                        id-slug (or bare id) of an existing note this one
                        revises
  --reason REASON       with --supersedes: why the old decision was revised
