[0m⚙ [0mexecute {"code":"const note2 = await tools.second_brain.create_note({\n  segment: \"work\",\n  project: \"seatap\",\n  title: \"Hono bodyLimit trusts Content-Length, so it cannot enforce a hard body cap\",\n  body: `Hono's built-in \\`bodyLimit\\` middleware short-circuits on \\`Content-Length\\` and only falls back to stream counting; a client can declare \\`Content-Length: 10\\` and stream 70 KB through undici. Verified in Source + official docs + a live probe.\nHosts do not save it: Node's HTTP parser bounds the wire body by the *declared* length, and the middleware never reads what the handler doesn't consume — so nothing hard-caps the actual bytes.\nFix used in Seatap M1b: a custom \\`/v1/*\\` guard that always counts the received stream into a bounded buffer, rejects >64 KiB with 413, and rebuilds the request; unconsumed overflow streams are cancelled (keep-alive handling left to Node).\nRelated trap: never derive rate-limit client identity from \\`X-Forwarded-For\\`/\\`CF-Connecting-IP\\` by default — use the peer socket (\\`@hono/node-server/conninfo\\`), otherwise header spoofing makes the throttle bypassable.`,\n  tags: \"hono,node,security,body-limit,gotcha,rate-limiting\",\n  links: \"01-Work/seatap/202610210343-seatap-m1b-abuse-protection-64-kib-body-cap-and-bounded-auth.md\",\n  memory_type: \"learning\",\n  entities: [\n    { name: \"Hono\", type: \"Technology\" },\n    { name: \"bodyLimit\", type: \"System\" },\n    { name: \"seatap\", type: \"Project\" },\n  ],\n});\nreturn note2;"} 
