[0m⚙ [0mexecute {"code":"const r = await tools.second_brain.create_note({\n  segment: \"career\",\n  type: \"learning\",\n  title: \"Allowlist-map attacker-controlled fields before they enter structured logs\",\n  body: `A review finding: an HTTP request record logged \\`c.req.method\\` directly. A field allowlist on the record does not make that safe — the raw method is attacker-controlled, so it can carry arbitrary (potentially secret-bearing) text into logs.\n\nFix pattern applied: map the raw value through a fixed set — \\`GET/HEAD/POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE/OPTIONS/CONNECT/TRACE\\` map to themselves, anything else becomes \\`OTHER\\`. The raw string is used only in the classifier, matched against static metadata, and never written out.\n\nVerification: a test sends a synthetic secret HTTP method and asserts the log shows \\`method: \"OTHER\"\\`, classification still works, and the marker is absent from the record.\n\nGeneral rule: for logging, enumerate the closed set of values you accept, not the shape of the input. Raw/untrusted data belongs behind a mapper, never in the sink.`,\n  memory_type: \"learning\",\n  entities: [\n    { name: \"Structured logging\", type: \"Topic\" },\n    { name: \"Request logging middleware\", type: \"System\" }\n  ],\n  relations: [\n    { source: \"Request logging middleware\", type: \"USES\", target: \"Structured logging\" }\n  ]\n});\nreturn JSON.stringify(r).slice(0,800);"} 
