[0m⚙ [0mexecute {"code":"const b = await tools.second_brain.create_note({\n  segment: \"work\",\n  project: \"inline-design\",\n  title: \"GB-scale uploads: sniff 512 bytes and stream writes, never buffer the body for sniffing\",\n  body: `In the inline-design Go backend the first single-shot ECW upload buffered the whole stream to sniff the file type, which OOMs at GB scale.\nFix: read only 512 bytes for magic sniffing (io.MultiReader/push-back), then stream the rest to disk with 1 MiB buffers — memory stays O(1) regardless of file size.\nRule: never let content-type detection own the request body; sniff a fixed prefix and pass the remainder through unread.\nAlso: single-shot multipart still hits proxy body limits for multi-GB files, so GB inputs must use the chunked session flow (POST /api/v1/uploads → PUT chunks with Content-Range → POST .../complete).\nEvidence: 1.8 GB case covered by session-math test (226 chunks, edge ranges) and O(1) streaming verified in a live 3 MB smoke run.`,\n  metadata: {\n    memory_type: \"learning\",\n    entities: [\n      {name: \"Inline Design\", type: \"Project\"},\n      {name: \"ECW\", type: \"Technology\"}\n    ],\n    relations: [\n      {source: \"Inline Design\", type: \"USES\", target: \"ECW\"}\n    ],\n    links: [\"01-Work/inline-design/202610282136-inline-design-now-has-a-go-ecw-upload-extraction-backend-rev.md\"],\n    tags: [\"go\", \"streaming\", \"uploads\", \"memory\", \"ecw\", \"gotcha\"]\n  }\n});\nreturn b;"} 
