[0m⚙ [0mexecute {"code":"const r = await tools.second_brain.create_note({\n  segment: \"work\",\n  project: \"inline-design\",\n  title: \"Inline Design browser DXF conversion swapped GeoJSON axis order, throwing overlays far off\",\n  body: `Reported symptom: upload -> convert -> open-in-map worked, but the DXF overlay rendered far from the basemap.\nRoot cause: client-side \\`utmToLL\\` returned Leaflet \\`[lat, lng]\\` and that pair was fed straight into GeoJSON \\`coordinates\\`, which require \\`[lng, lat]\\`; every point was mirrored. The Python precompute (server-side) was already \\`[lng, lat]\\` and aligned correctly, which masked the difference until the browser parser became the path.\nFix: swap the pair when emitting GeoJSON coordinates from the client converter.\nProof, not assertion: \\`viewer/dxf-overlay.check.js\\` executes the shipped conversion code against the real p26/p27 DXFs and diffs every feature against the server precomputed overlay. Before the fix 5/5 sampled coords were exactly swapped; after, 1696/1696 (p26) and 2089/2089 (p27) match with identical kind sequences. Kept in the repo as a permanent regression script (README note: keep the ordering honest).\nLive loop verified end to end: fresh 1.8 GB upload -> SUCCEEDED 1.43 GB GeoTIFF -> auto-tiles READY (17,223 tiles) -> raster meta 118500x113518, 0.2 m pixels -> both DXFs rendering via the \\`?job=\\` view.\nKnown wart: no DXF delete endpoint per the PRD's non-goals, so a duplicate p26 attachment shows twice in layer toggles; cosmetic only.`,\n  tags: [\"dxf\", \"geojson\", \"coordinate-order\", \"overlay\", \"leaflet\", \"regression-test\", \"inline-design\"],\n  memory_type: \"learning\",\n  entities: [\n    { name: \"inline-design\", type: \"Project\" },\n    { name: \"DXF\", type: \"Technology\" },\n    { name: \"GeoJSON\", type: \"Technology\" },\n    { name: \"Leaflet\", type: \"Technology\" },\n  ],\n  relations: [\n    { source: \"inline-design\", type: \"USES\", target: \"DXF\", quote: \"client-side utmToLL returned Leaflet [lat, lng] but fed it straight into GeoJSON coordinates\" },\n    { source: \"inline-design\", type: \"USES\", target: \"GeoJSON\", quote: \"that pair was fed straight into GeoJSON `coordinates`, which require `[lng, lat]`\" },\n  ],\n  links: [\"01-Work/inline-design/202610282342-inline-design-viewer-dxf-cad-overlays-align-via-the-geotiff-.md\"],\n});\nreturn JSON.stringify(r).slice(0, 1200);"} 
