[0m⚙ [0mexecute {"code":"const n1 = await tools.second_brain.create_note({\n  segment: \"personal\",\n  area: \"OpenCode\",\n  title: \"OpenCode TTS plugin: server-side speak-on-idle, TUI-only toggle, macOS say as default engine\",\n  memory_type: \"decision\",\n  tags: \"opencode,plugin,tts,voice-macOS-say\",\n  body: `Design for a text-to-speech OpenCode plugin: put speech in the **server** part so it works in TUI, web and remote sessions, and keep only the on/off toggle plus stop-playback keybind in the **TUI** part (optionally an RPC contract between them).\n\nSpeak on the \\`session.idle\\` event, read the last assistant message, strip Markdown, truncate to ~800 chars, then spawn a single engine binary with stdio ignored.\n\nToggle state lives in server storage (plugin \\`options\\` / \\`ctx.storage\\`) so commands and the TUI agree.\n\nDefault engine: macOS \\`say -v Samantha \"...\"\\` (free, offline, zero deps); stop playback with \\`pkill say\\`.\n\nKeep the engine pluggable — \\`say | piper | edge | openai-compatible\\` — so Piper or Kokoro can replace \\`say\\` later without rewriting the plugin.\n\nV2 plugin parts load from \\`@opencode/plugin\\` (server), \\`@opencode/plugin/tui\\`, \\`@opencode/plugin/rpc\\`; plugins are discovered in \\`.opencode/plugins/\\` or the \\`plugins\\` key of \\`opencode.jsonc\\`.`,\n  entities: [\n    {name: \"OpenCode\", type: \"System\"},\n    {name: \"macOS say\", type: \"Technology\"},\n    {name: \"Piper\", type: \"Technology\"},\n    {name: \"Kokoro-82M\", type: \"Technology\"}\n  ],\n  relations: [\n    {source: \"OpenCode\", type: \"USES\", target: \"macOS say\"},\n    {source: \"OpenCode\", type: \"INTEGRATES_WITH\", target: \"Piper\"}\n  ]\n});\nreturn JSON.stringify(n1).slice(0, 600);"} 
