[0m⚙ [0mexecute {"code":"const n = await tools.second_brain.create_note({\n  segment: \"personal\",\n  area: \"OpenCode\",\n  title: \"opencode-speak speaks only for the focused session — gate on `tui.session.select` / `session.viewed`, not on the turn event\",\n  memory_type: \"procedure\",\n  tags: \"opencode, opencode-speak, plugin, tts, session-focus, bugfix\",\n  links: \"00-Personal/OpenCode/202611011350-opencode-speak-overlap-fix-turn-events-carry-no-location-use.md, 00-Personal/OpenCode/202611011235-opencode-voice-s-llm-normalization-is-the-part-worth-reusing.md\",\n  entities: [\n    { name: \"opencode-speak\", type: \"Repository\" },\n    { name: \"OpenCode\", type: \"System\" }\n  ],\n  relations: [\n    { source: \"opencode-speak\", type: \"DEPENDS_ON\", target: \"OpenCode\" }\n  ],\n  body: `Symptom: every session's turns were spoken aloud, including background sessions (second-brain stop-filer sessions talking over the active one).\n\nFix: the server tracks the focused/viewed session from the TUI focus events \\`tui.session.select\\` and \\`session.viewed\\`; a completed turn is spoken only when its session equals that viewed session, otherwise it is skipped and logged with \\`reason=background\\`.\n\nFocus comes from the event, not from the turn event payload (turn events still carry no location — see the earlier dir=none finding).\n\nConsequence: opening a session in the TUI is enough to make it the speaking one.\n\nTradeoff: with two TUI windows only the most recently focused one speaks, because the gate is server-side and the viewed session is a single value. Per-window speech would require doing the gating TUI-side instead.`\n});\nreturn n;"} 
